    "user_text": "\"At the shutter you told Ora: west conduit, two turns, then the lift cage. Lose the lane cameras in the condensers.\" I keep my voice low. \"Did that route hold? I am asking you, not writing an answer for you.\"",
    "storyteller_text": "Niko\u2019s eyes narrow at the precision of the question. Outside, rain combs silver lines through the loading arcade; a lift cage rises behind him, slow enough to make every sealed container look accused.\n\n\u201cI told her that route because it was good for the first part of the night,\u201d he says. \u201cIt held through the west conduit and the condensers. No lane cameras caught us there. I watched Ora take the second turn.\u201d\n\nHe stops. The boundary arrives without performance.\n\n\u201cAfter that, we separated. Deliberately. I don\u2019t know whether she took the lift cage, doubled back, or found another exit. If I knew it, I wouldn\u2019t give it to you in a dispatch box.\u201d\n\nThe answer has less comfort in it than a lie would have. It is also less useful to anyone standing beyond the rain and trying to turn concern into a map.\n\n\u201cYou asked whether the route held,\u201d Niko continues. \u201cIt held as far as I saw it. I saw no public interdiction, no chase, no distress signal. That is not the same as saying Ora is safe. It means I won\u2019t invent an ending because you need one.\u201d\n\nKessa reaches beneath the dead-book sheet for her satchel. A small indicator on its clasp pulses once: an incoming welfare inquiry, ordinary Guild routing, unread. She glances at it, then turns the clasp facedown.\n\n\u201cYour friend has given you an honest limit,\u201d she says. \u201cNow give the dead the same courtesy.\u201d\n\nNiko looks at the sheet bearing Nera Voss\u2019s name. His expression does not soften, but it loses some of its defensive set.\n\n\u201cYou can keep asking me what I saw,\u201d he says. \u201cYou cannot make me carry Ora\u2019s half of the world into this room.\u201d\n\nUnder the amber lamp, the dead-book sentence waits in Mara\u2019s own hand or remains unwritten. Beside it, the Wickglass receipt remains a narrow, conditional piece of truth: an unnamed municipal adjunct asked how to isolate carriers through family obligations; Wickglass refused the question. Beyond the glass, the municipal-gray stranger is still only rain, a scheduler\u2019s recollection, and a direction toward canal stairs.\n\nKessa slides the pen toward you. It rolls once and comes to rest against the bronze token.",
